// §7 "Worth knowing". The lever here is the review, which is the only place
// on this cluster where a cleaning failure has a directly measurable dollar
// cost — and that makes the argument for itself without any claim about TLB.
// Brief §15. REWRITTEN for restaurants rather than moved: this is the page's
// BFD 14 section and its strongest argument, so the argument was worth keeping
// and only the examples had to change. The structure, the fifteen-minute
// framing and the closing "same way every single time" are all preserved; the
// examples move from cabin to dining room, and the final clause keeps the same
// move, specific knowledge of a specific room, in a restaurant register. It
// also pays off the demoted H1 line now sitting in the hero.
//
// The accommodation original is preserved in
// content-plans/HELD-holiday-park-and-cabin-content.md and should be reinstated
// if that content gets its own page.
const worthKnowingBody: TextBlockBody[] = [
  'Look at the one and two star reviews for any café, pub or restaurant in this region and count how many are about the room rather than the food. It is more of them than anyone expects. Not the menu, not the prices, not the service: a sticky table, a bathroom that had not been done since lunch, crumbs on the banquette, a smell near the bins. Each of those is a fifteen minute failure that sits on your listing for years and gets read by everyone deciding between you and the place across the road.',
  'The economics run the other way to how cleaning usually gets budgeted. Cutting the pre-open reset from forty minutes to twenty saves you a third of an hour of labour and costs you a review, and the review costs more than the year’s savings. Which is why the thing to buy is not the cheapest visit, it is a visit that happens the same way every single time, by somebody who has reset that room before and knows which table the afternoon sun hits.',
];

